00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:20.000
right, I am coming out
as blind to the world.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.210
And in turn, I'm coming out
as myself, I'm liberated,

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.650
I'm free, and this is me.

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:27.660
And it just felt like you were breathing

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:29.080
a sigh of relief.

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:32.260
- [Narrator] Being blind has
affected my mental health.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:33.710
- Strongly agree.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.050
- Strongly agree.

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:36.860
- Somewhat disagree.

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:38.180
- Strongly agree.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:39.310
- Strongly agree.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:40.373
- Strongly agree.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:45.000
- So having been born legally blind,

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:46.670
I never knew anything different.

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:48.840
It wasn't until the kids
become more aware and stuff,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:53.080
and poke out differences
and really nag on those,

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:56.220
that that's when I really
had that whole journey

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:58.440
of my mental health,

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.930
and being diagnosed with
mental illnesses and stuff.

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:03.330
But that really, I don't
blame my blindness.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:07.600
I blame the individuals that
may have really resulted

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:10.030
in my mental health deteriorating.

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:14.670
And it wasn't actually until,
so I got like a surgery.

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:17.930
It was experimental, I did regain vision

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:19.610
for the first time and it was considered

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:21.540
just visually impaired on the mark.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.580
And that only lasted for about two years.

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:25.700
And then overnight, it
all just deteriorated,

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:28.050
went back to how it was.

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:33.010
And I didn't find happiness
until after I went blind again.

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:34.160
I think it was 'cause

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:35.740
this is how I'm supposed
to experience life,

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:37.950
'cause I feel like there's
a greater purpose for it

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:39.900
in my life, and I got to
do something with that.

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:41.810
- James, I appreciate what you said.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:43.870
I thought that was quite
powerful because I hadn't thought

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:48.290
of it like that before, being
a person who has been in

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:51.620
several situations where
I was taken advantage of,

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:53.800
whether it was by a family member,

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:56.330
whether it was, you know,
a teacher molesting me

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:58.840
as a teenager, or even just more recently,

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:01.430
whether it's kids being
kids, and kids trying to,

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:04.050
you know, kind of get over
on their blind parent&gt;

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:05.900
Of course, I'm on it now.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:09.380
But all of those things,
you know, kind of bleed

00:18:10.258 --> 00:18:13.283
into one principle
theme that we are blind,

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:18.350
and sometimes you're gonna
go to those emotional

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:22.050
corners where I resent
being blind because of,

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:23.900
you know, what people might do

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:27.560
to, you know, weaponized my circumstance.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:29.240
So, you know, James,

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.700
you really said something
that stuck with me there.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:35.080
- Mario, I appreciate
how you phrased all that,

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:36.200
and shared all that.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:37.060
- It's hard, isn't it?

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:39.230
It's one of those things where
you look back and you think,

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:40.500
wow, why me?

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:42.270
I guess that's what I did at the start.

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:46.830
I was with my tub of Ben
and Jerry's, in my bed.

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:48.331
Yeah.
(laughing)

00:18:48.331 --> 00:18:49.370
And I always that girl,

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:52.470
I was the only girl in my
family, in my friendship group.

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:56.040
Everyone sighted, me, wishing
she had her sight back,

00:18:56.040 --> 00:19:00.770
and you know, my organ
died in front of my eyes.

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:04.140
Like, of course I'm gonna feel
low, you know what I mean?

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:06.160
- It is a grieving process.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:07.840
It's definitely not just vision loss.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:10.230
It's the loss of losing
a sense of self as well.

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:14.310
It's a loss of having hopes
and desires for a future that

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:16.220
might not be what it is.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:18.100
It might be better, but
it won't be what you

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:19.930
envisioned it to be originally.

00:19:19.930 --> 00:19:22.130
- Blind people are three times more likely

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:24.120
to deal with depression and anxiety.

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:28.210
So if you are blind and you
struggle with mental illness,

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:29.950
you don't have to feel alone.

00:19:29.950 --> 00:19:33.290
We statistically are more
susceptible to struggling with it.

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:34.840
And to me it makes sense, ultimately,

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:38.240
we're living in a world that
isn't designed or created for

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:40.920
us and we're making our way
and we're doing it and we're

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:43.886
killing it, but it does
make life in certain

00:19:43.886 --> 00:19:45.823
circumstances, more difficult.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:49.090
- [Narrator] I have
experienced discrimination.

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:50.440
- Somewhat agree.

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:51.820
- Strongly agree.

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:52.960
- Strongly agree.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:54.220
- Strongly agree.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:55.480
- Strongly agree.

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:56.543
- Strongly agree.

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:02.190
- Ooh, I was the only one
that said somewhat, hey!

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:03.760
- I wonder if the difference is,

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:05.830
given we all use mobility aids.

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:08.820
- Um, no, actually, there's
this intersectionality

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:12.500
between living my entire
life as a black woman,

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:15.820
and being, as I've only been
diagnosed with this disease

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:17.330
and lived with it for the last 10 years,

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:20.330
I can see a clear difference
between the discrimination of

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:24.180
being black, versus the
discrimination of being blind.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.960
When I tell people that I've
gone through something or face

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:29.460
discrimination as a black female,

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:33.020
sometimes I'm met with doubt or

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:34.620
kind of like pushing it to the side,

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:37.500
or it's in your head or it's not that bad.

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:40.270
Whereas I feel like people meet
me halfway when it comes to

00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.770
my vision loss, people find it admirable,

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:45.850
and kind of attached to it,
identity as inspirational.

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:48.070
It's a different energy and
it's a different temperature,

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:51.270
which is why I said,
somewhat, not strongly.

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:52.790
- As a blind black man.

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:55.630
I can say that I've
experienced discrimination

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:56.710
on both fronts.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:21:00.520
Unfortunately, you know, the
crux of me is really the arts.

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:02.280
I did try out for "American Idol".

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:03.710
There's several rounds of auditions

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:05.750
you have to go through to
get on these reality shows,

00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:07.300
and this is like the first level of it.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:10.260
There at my guitar, and
I sing my heart out,

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:13.883
and then the producer, he
stopped me and said, dude,

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:18.400
normally we really don't
let blind people through,

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:21.160
and even if we do, we don't
usually let them go so far,

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:23.170
'cause they can be so
difficult to deal with

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:24.350
during production.

00:21:24.350 --> 00:21:26.913
But man, you're so dope, I got to say yes.

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:29.503
- Is that supposed to be a compliment?

00:21:29.503 --> 00:21:31.590
- That type of stuff really can leave

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:32.810
you feeling unaccomplished,

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:35.640
because obviously you want
to be seen for your ability.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.020
You want to be seen for the
content of your character,

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:40.160
but unfortunately that
doesn't always happen,

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:44.270
and that can, a bigger plug
to an earlier conversation

00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:46.800
we had, that can sometimes be a catalyst

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:51.060
for a plunge into, you know,
momentary depression as well.

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:54.730
- I do feel like the bulk of
the discrimination that I face

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:56.090
is due to my guide dog.

00:21:56.090 --> 00:21:58.170
And it's only getting increasingly worse,

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.930
as the misuse of service dogs,

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:04.290
and the use of fake
service dogs increases.

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:07.010
I've been a guide dog
user for 13 years now.

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:09.550
And over the last five, year over year,

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.180
it gets worse and worse and worse,

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:13.470
and I face more and more
and more discrimination.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:15.020
- I strongly agree.

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:19.780
When I'm entering a taxi,
people see my guide dog,

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:22.280
and not the person who needs a ride.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:26.573
I've had someone spit
at me, and swear at me.

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:30.260
And you know, even though now I've found

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:33.003
great taxi drivers in Central London,

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:35.660
it still does scare me.

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:39.800
- With the job related stuff,
I've been in those interviews.

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:41.170
Everything seems to go well,

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:43.550
they're giving me an estimation of like,

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:46.160
when I'm going to hear back,
oh, it's gonna be next Tuesday.

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:49.530
Yeah, we even want, you to
maybe even head this department.

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:52.070
And then when you start
mentioning accommodations,

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:53.790
then the conversation sort of shifts,

00:22:53.790 --> 00:22:57.300
and then next thing I know I
get an automated, you know,

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:00.370
decline through email, 30 days later,

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:02.490
instead of hearing back on that Tuesday.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:05.280
- Oftentimes people approach and you know,

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:07.100
want to ask to take a photo.

00:23:07.100 --> 00:23:09.750
They'll always ask my husband like,

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:11.940
is it okay if I take a
photo with Christine?

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:12.880
There's just things like that

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:14.690
that I think a lot of
people don't think about.

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:17.430
I feel like when someone
asks somebody else

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:19.580
if they are allowed to
take a photo with me,

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:22.500
that I'm a lesser person, that, you know,

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:25.200
I cannot make my own independent decision.

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.190
- I know it's a sighted world,

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:29.600
but I always find it
amazing that because of the

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:32.340
preconceived notion of what
they think blind people should

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:35.180
look like, or shouldn't look
like, that I'll be honest,

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:37.237
I think it's ridiculous
that I even get things like,

00:23:37.237 --> 00:23:39.770
you know, there's no
way this guy is blind.

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:42.770
He's muscular, and it's
like, what are you saying?

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:43.603
- Yeah.

00:23:43.603 --> 00:23:45.070
- Blindness is quite rare,

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:48.420
and I think the main place
that people get their

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:50.320
opinion on what blindness must be like

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:52.130
is through entertainment.

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:54.920
Because I choose to put
myself out there online,

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:58.170
and I don't, quote, look blind,

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.160
or look the way people
think I'm supposed to

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:03.600
as a disabled woman, I get
a lot of, she's faking it.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:05.010
You know, there's whole Reddit threads,

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:07.140
there's whole blogs devoted to trying to

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:08.670
debunk my blindness.

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:10.880
And that's why representation
is so important.

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:12.840
And that's why videos like this one

00:24:13.807 --> 00:24:16.210
is so important to watch, to share,

00:24:16.210 --> 00:24:18.050
because we need the education to spread,

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:23.050
so that that ignorance is
combated with education.

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:25.870
- [Narrator] If I could
regain my sight with a cure,

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:27.370
I would want to.

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:28.900
- Strongly agree.

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:30.590
- Somewhat agree.

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:31.970
- Strongly disagree.

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:33.080
- Disagree.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:34.690
- Strongly agree.

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:35.523
- Disagree.

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:38.630
- If I was to get my sight back,

00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:40.720
I would love to see the
faces of the children

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:42.850
that I've adopted, that would be amazing.

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:44.510
- I said, disagree.

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:48.260
My answer, probably a good four years ago,

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:49.810
would have been the complete opposite.

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:51.480
I'm gonna have my wedding next year,

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:55.190
and there's so much of me
that wants to see Ollie's face

00:24:55.190 --> 00:24:58.480
when I'm walking down the
aisle, or my mom's face,

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:00.960
or my sister's face,
or my dad's face again,

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:04.780
because they have now
faded from my memory.

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:07.290
That does hurt me inside quite deeply.

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:10.713
And just thinking of the
milestones really in life.

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:13.350
Having kids with Ollie in the future.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.520
But I think the reason
that my answer is disagree

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:19.970
is because I have finally
come to accept who I am,

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:23.610
and the fact that I really
do deeply believe now

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:27.740
that life without sight
is truly beautiful.

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:29.480
When I embraced that, that's when

00:25:29.480 --> 00:25:30.670
I don't want my sight back,

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:34.570
I don't need it to feel
fulfilled, and to feel Lucy.

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:39.030
- I said disagree like Lucy,
for many of the same reasons.

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:41.750
At the beginning of my vision loss,

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:44.560
I dealt with a lot of anger, resentment,

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:46.780
I threw myself pity parties, asked why me?

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.110
All I wanted was my sight back.

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:51.040
In fact, I was literally
the youth ambassador

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:53.080
for a charity whose sole
purpose was to fundraise

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:54.540
to find a cure for my disease.

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:57.720
And along my self acceptance
and self love journey,

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:00.390
I realized how toxic
that mindset was for me.

00:26:00.390 --> 00:26:01.690
And that's where the social model

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:03.150
versus medical model comes in.

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:07.200
The medical model is the idea
that the answer to disability

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:11.350
is curing it, which puts really the onus

00:26:11.350 --> 00:26:12.500
on the disabled person.

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:15.110
We are the burden on society
and we need to change,

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:18.450
be fixed, be healed, in order to be whole,

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:20.180
or good enough, or fit into society.

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:22.020
Whereas the social model
of disability says,

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:25.170
actually the disabled
person is perfectly fine,

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:26.680
and they aren't the problem at all.

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:29.790
The real problem is
societies inaccessibility,

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:32.340
with our infrastructure
in terms of, you know,

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:33.880
the design of our buildings,

00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:35.650
and our physical space around us.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:38.920
But also our technology
and just everything we use

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:40.810
and interact with on a daily basis.

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:43.410
So for all of those reasons,
I don't want a cure.

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:46.380
- So have gone through a bit of a process

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:49.710
of trying to cure my sight,

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:51.020
back when I was about 11 years old,

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:53.850
feeling pressured to do
so in order to fit in.

00:26:53.850 --> 00:26:56.210
And it reverting back within two years,

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:57.570
I was never happy during those two years,

00:26:57.570 --> 00:26:58.670
'cause I wasn't doing it for me.

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:02.750
It really didn't feel like I
was myself until I was able to

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:04.360
really embrace my blindness.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.010
One in seven people in the
world have some form of

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:10.700
disability and that may only
increase as time goes on.

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:12.330
I know for me and the purpose that I'm

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:14.480
here on this earth to do, it's

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:17.040
to leave this world a more
accessible and accepting

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.010
place than I found it.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:21.490
- I answered, somewhat agree.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:23.710
Ideally, I think I would
want my vision back

00:27:23.710 --> 00:27:25.950
for like, maybe a couple days,

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:28.230
just so I can watch some foreign films.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:31.110
See what, you know, maybe
my friends and family

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:35.500
look like now, but then I
feel like I don't necessarily

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:39.340
want my full vision back forever,

00:27:39.340 --> 00:27:42.300
because I think I'm kind of at
the halfway point of my life,

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:44.280
where I've lived half
of my life with vision,

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:45.930
and half of it without.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:49.530
So it would be so different and difficult.

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:51.170
And I think losing my vision

00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:55.690
put me in a position where I was forced to

00:27:55.690 --> 00:27:58.130
think outside of the box, and create

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:01.360
opportunities for myself,
and be more courageous.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:03.640
But it would be kind of cool just to

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:05.423
see what cars look like nowadays.

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:08.840
- I've come more into myself,

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:10.320
and kind of like what Christine said,

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:12.160
there's a lot about all of our characters

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:13.610
that couldn't have been,
wouldn't have been,

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:16.609
had it not been the challenges
of losing our vision.

00:28:16.609 --> 00:28:18.340
And I feel like I could
take the last 10 years,

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:20.720
and the character development
and take that forward,

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:23.790
whether I'm visually impaired or sighted.

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:26.780
- Yeah. I don't think I would
be the person I am today

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:28.440
if I hadn't lost my vision.

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:30.910
But past that, you
know, I really work hard

00:28:30.910 --> 00:28:33.550
to be the coolest blind Dad ever.

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:35.370
My kids don't miss the beat.

00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:38.963
I'm able to chase and play
with my seven and 10 year old.

00:28:40.030 --> 00:28:42.400
I box with my teenagers.

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:44.470
They think I'm Superman 'cause they see me

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:46.300
work out all the time, and we play,

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:47.900
and we do this, and we do that.

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:52.660
I'm content just being super blind Mario.

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:53.660
- I don't know about any of you,

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:55.750
but sometimes when I
think about who I would be

00:28:55.750 --> 00:28:57.500
if I was sighted, like it's scares me.

00:28:57.500 --> 00:28:59.840
I'm like, I don't know
who that person would be.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:01.767
And I'm so happy with this
person, that I'm like,

00:29:01.767 --> 00:29:03.780
I don't want to see who she would be.

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:05.670
This person's good.
- Yeah.

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:06.620
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:08.340
I don't need to think about that girl.

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.590
- I wholeheartedly
respect everyone's journey

00:29:11.472 --> 00:29:12.305
and everyone's perspective.

00:29:12.305 --> 00:29:13.340
- Yeah.

00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:16.280
Definitely, blindness is a
spectrum and we're all so unique.

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:17.540
- No, this was great.

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:19.810
You guys, this is an amazing bunch.

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:21.540
I'm so honored to meet everybody

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:23.612
and just to hear perspectives.

00:29:23.612 --> 00:29:25.424
- Yeah.

00:29:25.424 --> 00:29:28.320
- Such a beautiful experience,
I loved hearing you guys.

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:29.340
- James waves his hand.

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:30.500
- Great talking to all of you!

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:32.230
- So nice meeting all of you.

00:29:32.230 --> 00:29:33.610
- I hope we can all stay in touch.

00:29:33.610 --> 00:29:35.645
- Yeah, sounds good, it was
nice talking to everybody.

00:29:35.645 --> 00:29:36.478
- Bye!

00:29:36.478 --> 00:29:37.850
- I'm gonna go to bed now guys.

00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:39.810
- Night, take care, everyone.

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:40.723
Stay safe.

